### What is the problem with unbounded queues?

Executors.newFixedThreadPool(n) internally creates a ThreadPoolExecutor with an unbounded LinkedBlockingQueue. Under sustained overload, tasks accumulate without limit. Each Runnable and its captured state occupy heap memory. Millions of queued tasks exhaust the heap.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class UnboundedDangerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // DANGEROUS: unbounded queue
        ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);
        // Internally: new LinkedBlockingQueue<>() → Integer.MAX_VALUE capacity
        for (int i = 0; i < 10_000_000; i++) {
            pool.submit(() -> {
                try { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
            });
        }
        // 10 workers, 10M tasks queued → heap fills → OOM!
    }
}
```

### How do bounded queues fix this?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class BoundedSafeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ThreadPoolExecutor pool = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
                10, 20, 60, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
                new LinkedBlockingQueue<>(500),   // BOUNDED
                new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());
        // Queue full + 20 workers busy → CallerRunsPolicy
        // Caller thread executes → natural back-pressure
        // Client sees higher latency but no OOM
    }
}
```

### What should a production rejection handler look like?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.util.concurrent.atomic.LongAdder;

public class ProductionHandlerDemo {
    private static final LongAdder rejectionCount = new LongAdder();

    static final RejectedExecutionHandler handler = (task, executor) -> {
        rejectionCount.increment();  // Prometheus counter

        System.err.println("REJECTED: queue=" + executor.getQueue().size()
                + " active=" + executor.getActiveCount()
                + " max=" + executor.getMaximumPoolSize());

        if (rejectionCount.sum() > 100) {
            // Alert PagerDuty: pool saturated
        }

        if (!executor.isShutdown()) {
            task.run();  // CallerRuns fallback
        }
    };
}
```

### How is this different from each built-in policy?

| Policy | Behavior | Production use |
|---|---|---|
| AbortPolicy | Throws RejectedExecutionException | When caller must know about rejection |
| CallerRunsPolicy | Caller executes the task | Best default: natural back-pressure |
| DiscardPolicy | Silently drops | NEVER (silent data loss) |
| DiscardOldestPolicy | Drops oldest queued task | Only for real-time dashboards |
| Custom | Metrics + logging + fallback | Always in production |

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Is CallerRunsPolicy always safe? Almost. The risk is that the caller thread (e.g., Tomcat's HTTP thread) blocks while executing the task, increasing request latency. But this is better than OOM.
- How do you monitor pool saturation? Track: queue size, active thread count, rejection rate, and task completion rate. Alert when rejection rate exceeds threshold.
